          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %li
                %a{:href => "#discussion"} Discussion
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An analog of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo ,
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,\otimes,I)
            requires composition morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom (A,B) \otimes Hom(B,C) \to Hom(A,C)
          %p and identity morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \to Hom(A,A)
          %p
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            satisfying the axioms of associativity and unitality. To weakly enrich, we wish to have the axioms of associativity and unitality hold up to coherent 2-morphisms. To accomplish this we can replace
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+bicategory"} monoidal bicategory
            which has sufficient structure to accommodate this. This describes one approach to weakly enriching in the 2-dimensional case. For higher values of n we need to replace
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            with a weak n-category.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A category weakly enriched in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+bicategory"} monoidal bicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            is called a
            = succeed "-bicategory." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            = succeed "-bicategory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            includes the data of an ordinary enriched category in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decategorification"} decategorification
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            must contain associator and unitor 2-morphisms in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            which fill the appropriate diagrams of composition and unit 1-morphisms. These 2-cells are required to satsify higher dimensional coherence axioms.
          %p
            Note that this should not be confused with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+enriched+in+a+bicategory"} category enriched in a bicategory
            which allows for multiple bases of enrichment. A
            = succeed "-bicategory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+2-category"} monoidal 2-category
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            of categories enriched in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
            see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} enriched (∞,1)-category
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-operad"} (∞,1)-operad
            theory see (
            %a{:href => "#Lurie"}> Lurie, def. 4.2.1.12
            ):
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℒℳ
                    %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{LM}^\otimes
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operad+for+modules+over+an+algebra"} operad for modules over an algebra
            regarded as an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-operad"} (∞,1)-operad
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+operators"} (∞,1)-category of operators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒜𝓈𝓈
                    %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Ass}^\otimes
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+operators"} (∞,1)-category of operators
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+operad"} associative operad
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒱
                      %mo ⊗
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒜𝓈𝓈
                      %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}^\otimes \to \mathcal{Ass}^\otimes
              exhibiting a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/planar+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-operad"} planar (∞,1)-operad
              %strong weak enrichment
              of an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}^\otimes
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-operads"} fibration of (∞,1)-operads
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo ⊗
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℒℳ
                      %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    q \colon \mathcal{O}^\otimes \to \mathcal{LM}^{\otimes}
            %p
              which exhibits
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              as a module over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒱
                      %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}^\otimes
              in that it is equipped with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} equivalences
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒱
                      %mo ⊗
                    %mo ≃
                    %msubsup
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi 𝔞
                      %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{V}^\otimes \simeq \mathcal{O}^\otimes_{\mathfrak{a}}
            %p and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ≃
                    %msubsup
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi 𝔫
                      %mo ⊗
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{C} \simeq \mathcal{O}^\otimes_{\mathfrak{n}}
                    \\,.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Lurie"}> Lurie, def. 4.2.1.12
            )
          %blockquote
            %p
              maybe better: weak
              = succeed "?" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensoring"} tensoring
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+enrichment"}> homotopical enrichment
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} enriched (∞,1)-category
          %h2#references References
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            \-bicategories are briefly explained in Section 4 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"}> Richard Garner
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nick+Gurski"} Nick Gurski
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0711.1761"} arXiv:0711.1761
          %p Section 4.2.1 of
          %ul#Lurie
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Algebra"} Higher Algebra
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Gepner"}> David Gepner
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rune+Haugseng"} Rune Haugseng
              = succeed "," do
                %em Enriched ∞-categories via non-symmetric ∞-operads
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.3178"} arXiv:1312.3178
          %h2#discussion Discussion
          %p
            This was originally at
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
          .query
            %p
              %em> Sebastian
              \: Is there a formal meaning of
              = succeed "?" do
                %em weak enrichment
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              \: Yes there is; indeed Clark Barwick is writing a huge book on this.
            %p
              %em> Sebastian
              \: If not, is there at least a method how to get the definition of a weak
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "-category?" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              \: that sounds harder! That’s like pushing a rock uphill. It’s easier to go down from weak to strict.
            %p
              %em> Sebastian
              \: Of course, I have recognised that there are actually different definitions of what a weak n-category should be… so to give my question a bit more precision: How do I get a definition of a weak
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "-category?" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              \: globular strict n-categories have been understood since time immemorial, or at least around 1963, and there is just one reasonable definition. Globular weak n-categories were defined in the 1990s by Michael Batanin, and his theory relates them quite nicely to the globular strict ones. But there is also a
              %em different
              definition of globular weak n-categories due to Penon. It had a mistake in it which has now been fixed.
            %p
              From the preface to
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/johnbaez/show/Towards+Higher+Categories"} Towards Higher Categories
            %p
              There is a quite different and more extensively developed operadic approach to globular
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+omega-category"} weak infinity-categories
              due to Batanin (Bat1, Str2), with a variant due to Leinster (Lein3). Penon (Penon) gave a related, very compact definition of infinity-category; this definition was later corrected and improved by Batanin (Bat2) and Cheng and Makkai (ChMakkai).
            %p You can get the references there.
            %p I think this discussion should be moved over to some page on n-categories, since it’s not really about bicategories.
